  <h1>Christmas and Christmas markets with your dog</h1>
  <p class="guide-lead">Lit-up wooden chalets, mulled wine, crowds in the narrow streets: a Christmas market is perfectly doable with your dog, as long as you know what each town says, and don't forget the holiday hazards at home. Here is what Coco Explore found on official sources (read on 05/11/2026), without inventing anything: when no rule is published, we say so. The city pages linked below are in French.</p>

  <h2>Christmas markets with your dog</h2>
  <p>Rules vary from town to town, and they are rarely posted on the markets' own websites. The dates below are for the 2026 edition; check them before you go.</p>

  <h3>Strasbourg</h3>
  <p>The city says that dogs on a leash are accepted in the aisles of all the markets (27 November to 24 December 2026), but that visiting with an animal is <strong>strongly discouraged on Saturdays and Sundays</strong> because of the crowds: it is better to come on a weekday, in the morning. Dogs are not allowed in food shops, and each business is free to accept animals or not. On public transport, they are not admitted on CTS buses but travel free on trams (small dogs in a bag or basket; larger ones on a leash and muzzled). <a href="/strasbourg-avec-son-chien">See the Strasbourg page (in French) →</a></p>

  <h3>Colmar</h3>
  <p>For the Christmas markets (23 November to 29 December 2026), we found <strong>no rule about dogs</strong> on the markets' official website or on the tourist office pages: ask the tourist office (+33 3 89 20 68 92). In Colmar, the municipal order requires a leash. TRACE buses only accept small dogs in a bag or basket, and the driver of the Christmas shuttles may refuse animals when it is busy. Coco's tip (not a rule): in very crowded pedestrian streets, keep your dog on a short leash. <a href="/colmar-avec-son-chien">See the Colmar page (in French) →</a></p>

  <h3>Mulhouse</h3>
  <p>The tourist office lists "Animals accepted" on its Christmas market page (place de la Réunion, 20 November to 24 December 2026, then 26 and 27 December), with no other condition published. A leash is required in public spaces, according to the city. <a href="/mulhouse-avec-son-chien">See the Mulhouse page (in French) →</a></p>

  <h3>Obernai and Sélestat</h3>
  <p>No rule about dogs is published for the Christmas markets of Obernai and Sélestat in the pages we read. In Sélestat, a leash is required for all dogs in town according to a 2017 municipal bulletin (to be checked against a recent order). <a href="/obernai-avec-son-chien">Obernai (in French) →</a> · <a href="/selestat-avec-son-chien">Sélestat (in French) →</a></p>

  <h3>Riquewihr, Kaysersberg, Eguisheim and Ribeauvillé</h3>
  <p>Here too, <strong>no leash rule or ban</strong> is published for the Christmas markets of Riquewihr (27 November to 20 December 2026), Kaysersberg (Fridays, Saturdays and Sundays of Advent, 27 November to 20 December 2026) and Eguisheim (27 November to 30 December 2026, closed from 24 to 26 December according to market directories); in Ribeauvillé, the medieval market (5 to 13 December 2026) has no dog rule that we could read. For the Christmas shuttles between Colmar, Kaysersberg, Riquewihr and Ribeauvillé, the rule for dogs is not published. Plan on a short leash and check with the tourist office. <a href="/riquewihr-avec-son-chien">Riquewihr (in French) →</a> · <a href="/kaysersberg-vignoble-avec-son-chien">Kaysersberg (in French) →</a> · <a href="/eguisheim-avec-son-chien">Eguisheim (in French) →</a> · <a href="/ribeauville-avec-son-chien">Ribeauvillé (in French) →</a></p>

