  <h2>Holiday dangers for your dog</h2>
  <p>Festive meals, decorations, plants: the house changes at Christmas, and some of the traps are serious. Here is what the veterinary and official sources we read say.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Chocolate.</strong> The Western France animal poison centre (CAPAE-Ouest) points out that the dog is the most affected species and that dark chocolate is far more toxic than milk or white chocolate. The first signs (thirst, vomiting, restlessness, tremors) can appear within the hour or only several hours later; in serious cases, heart problems, coma, even death. The University of Montreal veterinary hospital adds that there is no antidote but that the outlook is generally good with early treatment: call your vet quickly, stating the type and amount eaten.</li>
    <li><strong>Grapes and raisins.</strong> Toxic to dogs according to CAPAE-Ouest: raisins keep all their toxicity, and a bunch of fresh grapes can be fatal for a dog of around ten kilos. In serious cases, acute kidney failure appears within 48 to 72 hours. Think of cheese platters, brioches and cakes.</li>
    <li><strong>Poultry bones.</strong> A small piece of poultry meat is generally harmless, but pieces with bones are strongly discouraged: thin and sharp, they can get stuck in the oesophagus or intestine. Close the bin properly. (University of Montreal veterinary hospital; this source does not distinguish cooked from raw bones.)</li>
    <li><strong>Holly, mistletoe and poinsettia.</strong> Anses states that holly leaves and berries are toxic to pets (digestive, even neurological problems with many berries), that mistletoe leaves and berries are toxic, even deadly, and that chewing several poinsettia leaves or stems can cause digestive problems and excessive drooling. The Montreal veterinary hospital considers the toxic dose of holly very low (two berries can cause symptoms in a medium-sized dog).</li>
    <li><strong>Garlands, ribbons and wrapping.</strong> They can be swallowed and block the digestive tract. According to the same source, if your pet has just swallowed a foreign object, call a vet before making it vomit or pulling on it; keep decorations out of reach.</li>
    <li><strong>Other table traps.</strong> Xylitol (a sweetener in "sugar-free" products) requires an urgent consultation whatever the amount, and sweet alcoholic drinks or raw bread dough can poison a dog (same source).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If your dog has swallowed something, Anses recommends calling a veterinary poison centre (Lyon +33 4 78 87 10 40, 24/7 according to Anses; Nantes +33 2 40 68 77 40, 8:30 am to midnight according to CAPAE-Ouest) and keeping the label or a photo of the plant. Numbers and steps are detailed in our guide (in French).</p>
  <p class="cat-see-more"><a href="/guide-urgence-veterinaire-chien">Read the guide "Finding an on-call vet on holiday" (in French) →</a></p>

  <h2>Fireworks and New Year</h2>
  <p>The SPA (French animal welfare society) points out that the loud, sudden noise of fireworks frightens dogs and can make them run away, especially at New Year. Its advice:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>keep your dog indoors, not in the garden, with doors, windows and shutters closed, and soft music or the television to mask the noise;</li>
    <li>if you have to go out with it, use a short leash (rather than a retractable one), keep its identification up to date (tag, microchip or tattoo), and avoid the firing area just before and after;</li>
    <li>do not take a puppy with you: the stress can traumatise it;</li>
    <li>if your dog is very anxious, ask your vet for advice.</li>
  </ul>

